<%# People, on PEOPLE.md's argument — and its named design trap. The page
    has to actively disclaim CRM-for-friends, because that is what every
    reader will assume a "contacts" feature is. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    The wealthy have always had social infrastructure. Someone who remembers
    every birthday and sends the gift. Clubs that manufacture friendship.
    Somebody to make the gathering actually happen, rather than leaving it
    at "we should do this more often" for nine years.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most people get a contacts app that is effectively a graveyard, and a
    feed engineered so that scrolling <em>about</em> people substitutes for
    time <em>with</em> them. The result is a slow, unremarked loss of the
    relationships that would have mattered most, to nobody's decision in
    particular.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Not a database of humans</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The obvious way to build this is customer-relationship software pointed
    at your friends, and it is the wrong way — the moment the core activity
    becomes enriching contact records, you've built something that makes
    people into pipeline. So the rule here is that every feature has to end
    in contact: a message sent, a date on the calendar, a visit that
    happened. Not in a tidier record.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Which is also why keeping it up to date is meant to cost almost nothing.
    You log that you saw someone in about four seconds, and the app does the
    filing.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("people") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you see</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Your circle, not your address book") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The people you'd regret losing touch with, surfaced — separate from
        the three hundred acquaintances a phone sync drags in. The full
        roster still exists; it just isn't what greets you.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Who you haven't spoken to") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The friendship that's drifted eleven months without either of you
        deciding anything. Named gently, with something you could actually
        send, rather than a guilt counter.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Groups, not just individuals") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Church, the club, the team, the mutual aid group, the school
        parents. Real social life is mostly groups, and an app that only
        understood one-to-one friendships would recreate the isolation it's
        meant to answer.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "What you know about them") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The allergy, the new job, the name of their kid, what they said
        about their mother last spring. The things a thoughtful person
        remembers, kept somewhere that isn't only your memory.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">For the hard ones too</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Not every conversation you're avoiding is a cheerful one. The apology
    you owe, the boundary you've never managed to state, the friend whose
    parent just died and whom you haven't messaged because you don't know
    what to say. Those are the moments people most need a hand with, so
    they're here rather than treated as outside the remit.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Who can see it</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Yours alone by default. What you've recorded about a friendship is a
    genuinely private thing, and it stays that way unless you share it —
    the household calendar can carry the dinner without the household
    reading your notes about the guest.
  </p>
</section>
